@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: keep one Proveo host while running sbx locally or on a cloud worker
' covers: internal/run/choices.go
' covers: internal/run/topology.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/topology.go
title PLANNED — One Proveo host for local and cloud sandboxes

node "Operator's machine\nProveo host" {
  component "Proveo\nlaunch, observe, clean up" as CLIENT
  database "Workspaces, run receipts,\nlogs and recovered artifacts" as STATE
  node "Local sbx daemon\nand microVMs" as LOCAL
}
node "Ubuntu cloud instance\nexecution worker" {
  component "SSH endpoint\nand local sbx daemon" as RUNNER
  folder "Staged workspace\nand run-scoped files" as WORK
  node "sbx microVM\nagent and tools" as VM
}

CLIENT ARROW_MAIN STATE : record ownership and results
CLIENT ARROW_MAIN LOCAL : local run and cleanup
CLIENT ARROW_MAIN RUNNER : SSH lifecycle commands and terminal I/O
STATE ARROW_MAIN WORK : stage inputs and return results
RUNNER ARROW_MAIN VM : create, attach, stop, remove
WORK ARROW_MAIN VM : mount or clone on the worker

note bottom of CLIENT
  **Desired behavior**
  The operator's machine is always the Proveo host.
  It owns launch, receipts, status, artifact recovery
  and cleanup for local and cloud sandboxes. The
  operator cleans up from this one machine, including
  after a mixed local and cloud session. Proveo reaches
  each cloud worker over SSH to remove that run's
  sandbox, staged files and run-scoped access.
end note

note bottom of STATE
  Record the backend, worker identity and sandbox
  identity so restart and cleanup use the same target.
  Recover the requested refs and artifacts before
  removing the workspace. If SSH drops during cleanup,
  retain the receipt and show cleanup as pending.
  Retry uses the same identities.
end note

note bottom of RUNNER
  The cloud OS supplies KVM and runs sbx. Its daemon
  applies the egress policy and credential grants for
  the run. The workstation selects and scopes those
  grants. Remote sbx sees paths on the worker, so the
  workstation stages the intended Git revision or
  worktree before launch. Start by proving the flow on
  Ubuntu bare metal with working KVM. Test nested KVM
  on a candidate instance with sbx diagnose and a real run.
end note

legend bottom
  **Current preview**
  The disabled cloud option in the OS row previews the SSH hop. The grid fits the short
  name and the hint says coming soon. Local sbx is the active execution path.

  **Research refreshed 2026-11-18**
  * Self-hosted SSH worker. Docker documents sbx workspace paths and the daemon's host-side
    proxy. Its local SSH integration relays through the daemon socket. The community report
    demonstrates invoking that proxy over SSH from another machine. The lifecycle of a
    workstation-owned Proveo run over this path still needs a live trial.
    https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/architecture/
    https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/integrations/#how-ssh-connections-work
    https://github.com/docker/sbx-releases/issues/523#issuecomment-5456158079
  * Docker-managed cloud. The workstation can issue lifecycle commands through the
    sbx --cloud API and use the documented cloud SSH endpoint for interactive access.
    This uses a separate state and credential store. Workspaces use clone or file transfer.
    Evaluate it separately against the same workstation-owned lifecycle.
    https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/cloud/local-vs-cloud/
    https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/cloud/usage/
  * KVM on a self-hosted worker. Docker documents the KVM requirement. Its nested-KVM
    request remains open. AWS and Compute Engine document nested KVM on supported instances.
    https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/
    https://github.com/docker/sbx-releases/issues/425
    https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/amazon-ec2-nested-virtualization.html
    https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/nested-virtualization/overview

  **Verification before enabling Cloud**
  From one workstation, launch a local sandbox and a cloud sandbox. Reconnect to the
  cloud session over SSH. Recover a Git ref and an artifact. Disconnect SSH during
  cleanup, confirm the pending run remains visible, then reconnect and finish both
  cleanups from the workstation. Verify scoped cleanup preserves other runs on the worker.
endlegend
@enduml
